speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, today it is again my duty to present a petition concerning the Government's plan to tax food, and I am delighted to hear that the Conservative Caucus also objects to this tax on food and has joined us in trying to persuade the Minister of Finance (Mr. Wilson) to change his mind and drop this regressive concept. The petition reads as follows:
Whereas since September, 1984, the federal Government has imposed on Canadian taxpayers 25 new taxes or tax increases, in particular on medical and dental supplies, snack foods, candy and soft drinks;
Whereas the federal Government in its tax reform proposal has studied the possibility of imposing on Canadians a general tax on goods and services, including food;
And whereas food constitutes a vital element of day-to-day life and imposition of this tax would have the effect of seriously penalizing certain categories of taxpayers, such as the aged, families, the unemployed, low income taxpayers, youths and students;
Wherefore your petitioners humbly pray and call upon Parliament to reject all measures that would have the effect of imposing a sales tax on food products.
